{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive 'methodological rule' akin to choosing the sharper sword: it maximizes testability (informational content) without asserting future success probability.","counterconditions":["The agent must accept that this preference offers no guarantee of practical success (the bridge might still fall).","The distinction between 'acting on' and 'believing true' must be rigorously maintained."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Corroboration reports past performance in severe tests; preferring the theory with higher informational content (lower logical probability) is rational because it exposes itself more to falsification. If it survives, we have learned more, even if we cannot predict it will survive the next test.","optional_refs":["66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","354fa70c0e0698475f3990a6611ddf1b45577f6071bdf6fcf8f9490f23ab5688"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because any decision to 'act on' a theory inherently assumes the uniformity of nature (that the future will resemble the past), which Popper's logic cannot escape despite his verbal denials.","counterconditions":["Popper must admit that 'rational action' requires more than just 'having tested', it requires 'expecting continuity'.","The distinction between 'psychological habit' and 'logical justification' collapses when the stakes involve physical safety."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Action implies expectation. To build a bridge using Theory A over Theory B because A survived tests yesterday is to assume that the laws governing the bridge's stability tomorrow will be the same as yesterday. This assumption is the principle of induction.","optional_refs":["6d2476f30f531f1d40759d5aa09b80